# TP-Link Archer T2U Nano `2357:011e` on Ubuntu Server 24.04 / Kernel 6.8

Working fix for **TP-Link Archer T2U Nano AC600** on **Ubuntu Server 24.04.4 LTS** with **kernel 6.8.0-139-generic**, using the `lwfinger/rtw88` DKMS driver.

---

## Tested environment

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Ubuntu Server | 24.04.4 LTS |
| Kernel | 6.8.0-139-generic |
| Adapter | TP-Link Archer T2U Nano |
| USB ID | `2357:011e` |
| Chipset family | RTL8811AU / RTL8821AU |
| Secure Boot | Disabled |

The final working solution was the **`lwfinger/rtw88` DKMS backport**, which provides the `rtw_8821au` module.

---

## 1. Identify the adapter

Run:

```bash
lsusb
```

Mine showed:

```text
ID 2357:011e TP-Link AC600 wireless Realtek RTL8811AU [Archer T2U Nano]
```

Before installing a driver, also check whether Linux already created a Wi-Fi interface:

```bash
iw dev
ip link
```

In my case, there was no wireless interface initially.

---

## 2. What did not work

### Ubuntu `rtl8812au-dkms` package

I first tried:

```bash
sudo apt install -y rtl8812au-dkms
sudo modprobe 8812au
```

The module loaded, but the adapter never appeared in:

```bash
iw dev
```

Checking the driver aliases showed that the packaged module did not include the exact USB ID `2357:011e`:

```bash
modinfo 8812au | grep -i 2357
```

The package was removed:

```bash
sudo apt remove -y rtl8812au-dkms
```

---

### Another `rtl8812au` GitHub driver

I also tried another `rtl8812au` repository that appeared to list the adapter as supported.

The source contained the device entry:

```text
2357:011e
```

but the RTL8821A path was disabled in the Makefile:

```text
CONFIG_RTL8821A = n
```

After enabling it, the build failed because required RTL8821A source files were missing.

That path was abandoned.

---

### Legacy `8821au-20210708` driver

I then tried the dedicated RTL8811AU / RTL8821AU driver.

It recognized `2357:011e` as a supported device, but on Ubuntu 24.04 / kernel 6.8 it failed during DKMS compilation because of `cfg80211` API type mismatches.

Representative errors:

```text
error: passing argument 1 of 'cfg80211_new_sta' from incompatible pointer type
error: passing argument 1 of 'cfg80211_del_sta' from incompatible pointer type
```

Several `cfg80211_ops` callbacks also expected:

```text
struct net_device *
```

while the driver supplied:

```text
struct wireless_dev *
```

That driver was removed before continuing.

---

## 3. Working solution: `lwfinger/rtw88`

Install the build requirements:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y git dkms build-essential linux-headers-generic iw
```

Create a source folder:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/src
cd ~/src
```

Clone the driver:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lwfinger/rtw88.git
cd rtw88
```

Install it through DKMS:

```bash
sudo dkms install $PWD
```

On my system, DKMS successfully built and installed modules including:

```text
rtw_core
rtw_usb
rtw_8821a
rtw_8821au
```

Verify:

```bash
dkms status
```

You should see an installed `rtw88` entry for your running kernel.

Install the firmware:

```bash
sudo make install_fw
```

Copy the module configuration:

```bash
sudo cp rtw88.conf /etc/modprobe.d/
```

Then reboot:

```bash
sudo reboot
```

---

## 4. Verify after reboot

Run:

```bash
iw dev
```

The adapter finally appeared as a managed wireless interface:

```text
phy#0
    Interface wlx40ed008a623c
        ifindex 3
        wdev 0x1
        addr 40:ed:00:8a:62:3c
        type managed
```

Also check:

```bash
ip link
```

The interface appeared as:

```text
3: wlx40ed008a623c: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> ...
```

At this point the driver is working.

The interface may still show `state DOWN` until it is brought up and connected to a Wi-Fi network.

---

## 5. Bring the interface up

Use your actual interface name:

```bash
sudo ip link set wlx40ed008a623c up
```

Then scan:

```bash
sudo iw dev wlx40ed008a623c scan | grep SSID
```

My Wi-Fi network appeared successfully in the scan, confirming the adapter and driver were operating correctly.

---

## 6. Why the USB ID matters

With USB Wi-Fi adapters, the marketing name alone is not enough.

Always check:

```bash
lsusb
```

and research Linux support using the exact **vendor:product ID**.

For this adapter:

```text
2357:011e
```

is the useful identifier.

---

## 7. Avoid multiple competing out-of-tree drivers

Before trying another driver, check:

```bash
dkms status
```

Remove failed or conflicting DKMS modules before installing another one.

Multiple drivers attempting to claim the same Realtek adapter can make troubleshooting much harder.

---

## 8. Kernel updates

Because this installation uses DKMS, the module should be rebuilt automatically when compatible new kernels are installed.

After a major kernel update, it is still worth checking:

```bash
dkms status
iw dev
```

---

## Short version

For:

```text
Ubuntu Server 24.04.4 LTS
Kernel 6.8.0-139-generic
TP-Link Archer T2U Nano
USB ID 2357:011e
```

the working path in my case was:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y git dkms build-essential linux-headers-generic iw

mkdir -p ~/src
cd ~/src

git clone https://github.com/lwfinger/rtw88.git
cd rtw88

sudo dkms install $PWD
sudo make install_fw
sudo cp rtw88.conf /etc/modprobe.d/

sudo reboot
```

After reboot:

```bash
iw dev
ip link
```

The Wi-Fi interface appeared successfully.

---

## Why I am sharing this

This took several driver attempts and a fair amount of troubleshooting to isolate.

I am posting it so the next person with the same USB ID and Ubuntu/kernel combination has a much shorter path to a working system.

If your kernel, adapter revision, or USB ID is different, verify support before assuming the exact same steps will work.
